On that special day, Kent Paul Dolan, (xanthian@well.com) said...

> Trying to find some part of the
> day when I can pack to move, is "almost impossible"
> when chronic massive depression leaves me doing
> anything useful less than ten minutes of any given
> hour. 

Try some good uplifting music, that might brighten you up and give you 
the courage to at least start things. Don't command yourself to finish 
them until a deadline, and for gods sake, don't tell your self how much 
is still left to do, discouraging yourself. 

Or if you can't move, start preparing the move, eg by just taking a 
notebook, labeling pages with "clothing" "boxes and bags", "body care", 
and listen what you can currently remember of your household stuff. it 
won't be complete. Next day, fill in another page. After half a week, 
review the first one. You will find you left something out. Add it. 
Imagine how things fit best into a given restricted space. Just "play" 
with the dimensions, don't do it practically. 

After some time, "a map coalesces in your mind", how things might be 
arranged. Try part of it, only some big pieces, and check how much can 
be stuffed into the gaps in between (amd be it your socks in a plastic 
bag). 

Finally you'll have found a pattern that works, and that doesn't to have 
any systematical meaning ("but the toothbrush has to be near the 
towel"), but makes good use of the space. Keep it. I made the 
experience, that this method allows for a lot of creative, yet working 
organisation. The only problem is: You'll have to disassemble this mess 
when you have arrived in the new place. Allow several days for putting 
all things into their proper places, it will finally happen, because 
otherwise you cannot reach your comb, or whatever. 

I have been a student, and moved my stuff twice each semester, to the 
university and back home, and the distance was about 450 miles. 
Everything I needed had to fit into a small car, except of the 
furniture. It worked. 


